### HD-187: hand-written node-exporter textfile metrics used to exercise the backup alert
### rules in the DEV observability stack (docker-compose.observability.dev.yml). The
### directory is tracked (via .gitkeep) because the bind mount needs it to exist; the
### samples people drop in it are throwaway.
observability/textfile/*.prom

### HD-186 load harness (ops/loadtest/). Three kinds of file that must never be committed,
### and the reasoning differs for each:
###
###   config.env   holds the load password AND its bcrypt hash. A hash in a source-available
###                repository whose plaintext is beside it is a credential, not a digest.
###                The REAL configuration now lives outside the repository entirely
###                (/opt/hamstrack/.loadtest.env — ops/ is a synced path, so a file here is
###                copied into deploy backups and raises the drift check). These patterns
###                stay for a local rehearsal copy, and cover the shapes an editor or a
###                hand-copy leaves behind: a `.bak` beside a secret is still the secret.
###   tokens.json  a live credential file for the whole load pool (~227 accounts at the
###                shipped ladder), minted in pre-flight. It lives on the GENERATOR only,
###                and its home is now OUTSIDE the repository — $LOAD_RESULTS_DIR, the same
###                reasoning that moved config.env and results/, applied at last to the one
###                file that is unambiguously a credential rather than a configuration.
###                DELETING IT REVOKES NOTHING — it is the client's copy, the refresh tokens
###                in it live 30 days server-side, the ACCESS tokens in it are accepted on a
###                signature check alone for another 30 minutes, and the accounts keep
###                working with the shared password. Revocation is a server-side act:
###                ops/loadtest/fixture/revoke.sh, run on the box.
###
###                The patterns below are the belt to that brace, and they are UNANCHORED on
###                purpose. `ops/loadtest/**/tokens.json` covered the old default location
###                and nothing else: a file minted from the repository root — which is where
###                somebody stands to run `k6 run ops/loadtest/k6/mint-tokens.js` — landed
###                at ./tokens.json and was NOT ignored. A credential file is not a thing to
###                ignore by path.
###   results/     run output: k6 summaries, actuator scrapes, pg samples, fingerprints.
###                RESULTS-<date>.md is the curated record and IS committed; the raw capture
###                it was written from is large, and is attached to the ticket instead. The
###                capture is scrubbed by construction — `url` is out of k6's system tags
###                (ops/loadtest/k6/lib/config.js) because SSE authenticates by query
###                parameter, so a default capture would carry live access tokens.
ops/loadtest/config.env
ops/loadtest/config.env.*
!ops/loadtest/config.env.example
ops/loadtest/**/*.loadtest.env
tokens.json
tokens.json.*
**/tokens.json
**/tokens.json.*
ops/loadtest/**/*.bak
ops/loadtest/results/

### HD-187 backup configuration. The real file lives at /etc/hamstrack/backup.env and
### carries AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, so nothing here should ever be committed — but the
### install line in backup.env.example is run from THIS directory, and an operator who
### copies rather than installs lands `ops/backup/backup.env` in the tree. That is the
### same shape as ops/loadtest/config.env: a secret inside `ops/`, which a deploy syncs
### wholesale. apply-config.sh refuses it either way (it scans the release tree, and an
### ignored file is still a file), so this block is the second belt, not the guard.
### The template must keep travelling — a `<name>.env.example` is the instructions for
### the file being refused — hence the negation.
ops/backup/backup.env
ops/backup/backup.env.*
!ops/backup/backup.env.example

### HD-175 — the contrast audit's own output. Not committed: it carries the DOM
### of a real, logged-in workspace (issue titles, member names) and it is a
### measurement of one instance at one moment, not a design artefact. The
### reviewable outputs are the token values in index.css and the numbers quoted
### on the ticket. The report also carries its own "_warning" key, because an
### ignore rule protects a repository and protects no ticket, chat thread or
### email — and the file is what gets pasted into those.
###
### THREE patterns, unanchored, for the reason recorded above for tokens.json: an
### output file is not a thing to ignore by path. `src/main/frontend/audit/*.json`
### alone was checked by reading rather than with `git check-ignore`, and it
### ignored the default report while missing every other place the script really
### writes — `*` does not descend, so `audit/out/report.json` and
### `audit/nested/deep/r.json` were tracked, and AUDIT_REPORT=audit/out/r.json is
### a working invocation because the script mkdirSync's the parent: it CREATES a
### directory inside the directory this rule exists to protect and leaves the
### report in it. The repo root is missed the same way, and that is where somebody
### stands when they run `npm run audit:contrast`. Verify a change here by running
### `git check-ignore -v <path>` on each of those four, not by reading the globs.
**/contrast-report.json
src/main/frontend/audit/*.json
src/main/frontend/audit/**/*.json
